{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795259353,"nonce":"Zz6hhYbx5aJ2I6HrtzH8h/xFp6A=","message":"Principle, set 2026-11-21: data-oriented throughout, GPU first, everything frame rendered. The GPU draws everything every frame; no CPU-rasterized or CPU-composited surfaces are cached between frames. A CPU path exists only where the GPU cannot do the work, and is documented as an exception.\n\nWhat follows from it:\n- Component storage is laid out to be uploaded as-is: struct-of-arrays in GPU-friendly formats (packed colours, half floats where precision allows), written to persistent or ring buffers, uploading only changed ranges. Data is not duplicated between CPU and GPU when one copy suffices.\n- Transforms, culling, particle simulation and skinning run as compute where the device has `Compute` (Metal, Vulkan, Direct3D 12, WebGPU). On GLES and WebGL2, which lack compute, the same work moves into vertex shaders where it can; otherwise it falls back to the CPU, selected by hxn-gpu's feature flags.\n- Sprites, shapes, glyphs and UI quads are instanced draws from those buffers, batched per frame-graph pass; no per-object draw calls.\n- Vector shapes render with shaders (signed distance fields or GPU tessellation), not CPU path rasterization.\n- Text: shaping and line breaking stay on the CPU (hxn-text over HarfRust) and run only when text changes; glyphs are rasterized once into an atlas, preferring SDF/MSDF so scaling needs no re-rasterization; drawing is GPU instancing every frame.\n- UI layout runs only when something changes; the UI is still drawn each frame by the GPU, never composited into CPU-side textures.\n- Video frames from hxn-av become GPU textures, imported without copying where the platform allows.\n- The frame graph aliases transient render targets to reduce GPU memory.\n\nKnown CPU exceptions: text shaping and layout, audio mixing (hxn-audio), asset decoding and cooking, gameplay logic, knowledge-graph queries, and physics until a GPU path is designed.","files":null}]}